2.02 release notes (24/08/26)

This release substantially restructures the implementation around a documented
high-integrity C coding profile. Protocol state is now instance-owned and uses
an explicit I/O interface, with POSIX terminal and descriptor operations
isolated in a platform adapter. Protocol and file I/O failures are checked and
reported consistently, including broken output pipes and delayed file-write
errors.

The C99 type model has been tightened throughout. Wire values and constants use
matching exact-width unsigned types, conversions at platform boundaries are
checked, and proven non-aliasing contracts are documented with carefully
limited use of restrict. Generated immutable slicing-by-4 tables retain the
CRC32 performance improvement without mutable initialization state.

Verification now includes 66 end-to-end behaviour tests, focused protocol and
POSIX adapter tests, strict Clang and GCC diagnostics, static analysis,
clang-tidy, address and undefined-behaviour sanitizers, fuzz smoke testing and
structural coverage. The automated gates require at least 90 percent branch
coverage and 100 percent MC/DC coverage for applicable decisions, and CI tests
both compilers on Linux and Clang on macOS. These measures improve assurance;
they do not constitute safety qualification or compliance with an external
coding standard.

Protocol I/O performance has been improved with bounded buffered writes,
read-ahead input and split fast paths for common receive-side decoding. A
double close when the receiver skipped an existing file has also been fixed,
and its formerly timing-dependent regression test is now deterministic.

2.01 release notes (24/08/26)

This maintenance release improves packaging and documentation without
changing ZMODEM transfer behaviour. The makefile now provides install,
install-strip and uninstall targets with staged installation through DESTDIR.
GNU and BSD path, installation and stripping conventions are supported, while
compiler, linker, hardening, library and test-interpreter settings remain
overridable by packagers. Binaries and manual pages install with conventional
0555 and 0444 permissions.

The programmer-oriented ZMODEM protocol reference has been renamed to
ZMODEM.md and carefully converted to GitHub-flavoured Markdown. It now has a
linked table of contents, structured headings, packet diagrams, tables, code
blocks and compatibility and security callouts. Obvious transcription errors
were corrected while the license and special acknowledgement remain intact.

A GitHub Pages site now provides a concise introduction to the tools and their
history, including the 2015 transfer of the original author's copyright. The
README is shorter and links to the standalone license instead of reproducing
it in full.

2.00 release notes (24/08/26)

This release incorporates applicable bug fixes from the Synchronet ZMODEM
implementation which originally descended from this code. Protocol recovery,
acknowledgement handling, resume positions, end-of-file processing and error
reporting are substantially more robust. A comprehensive protocol regression
suite now covers successful transfers as well as malformed and hostile input.

Three remotely triggerable receive-side buffer overflows present in releases
1.02 and 1.03 have been fixed. Both CRC16 and CRC32 data subpackets are bounded
and fully consumed before oversized packets are rejected. Incoming file names
and metadata are validated before use, and exact filename boundaries are
tested. Related integer conversion and character classification edge cases
have also been hardened.

ZedZap 4 KiB and 8 KiB subpackets are supported with the -4 and -8 options.
The new -s option provides non-streaming operation, and -w limits the amount
of unacknowledged data with a sliding transmit window. The sender also adapts
to receiver buffering and overlap capabilities and adjusts its subpacket size
in response to transfer errors.

Performance has been improved with buffered transmit escaping, larger input
buffers and batched slicing-by-4 CRC32 calculation on both transmit and
receive paths. These changes reduce per-byte overhead while retaining CRC
validation and protocol synchronization for malformed or oversized packets.

1.03 release notes (24/08/26)

Thirty-two years is probably long enough between releases. The sources are
now under the 2-clause BSD license, with the old third-party notices kept
intact. OpenBSD portability changes have been incorporated and the build now
uses the compiler selected by make. The obsolete Unite! and SunOS 4 code has
been removed in favour of strict C99 and POSIX.1-2001 interfaces.

Compiler and linker errors have been fixed and the code builds without
warnings from modern versions of Clang and GCC. Protocol bytes, CRC values,
file sizes and offsets now use exact-width unsigned types with explicit wire
byte order. Large-file offsets are requested where available. The README is
now Markdown and GitHub Actions builds the sources and packages releases.

1.02 release notes (18/07/94)

Crash recovery now supported and the file date is set (which didn't happen
so far.) Added a CREDITS file. Mr. Paul Vixie (paul@vix.com; moderator
of comp.sources.unix) is currently reviewing the source in order to resolve
the copyright mess. Shouldn't be a problem.

1.01 release notes (17/07/94)

Wow, how's that, to be accussed of stealing copyrighted code by Mr. Forsberg
himself... (well, it did cause the release to be retracted from 
comp.sources.unix it's a shame). Fortunately it isn't true as anyone (except
maybe Mr. Forsberg) can see for themselves. It seems we now have manual pages
which were contributed by Bart Massey (bart@cs.uoregon.edu).
to avoid problems in passing the file through ms-dos machines the archive
now goes as zmXXX.zip where XXX is the version number. So far no technical
changes; only a marked failure to communicate with sz and rz has been noted.
Anybody interested in fixing this is welcome to do so; please mail us the 
changes and they will be incorporated in the next release. I would like to
express my surprise at the ease with which the moderator of a usenet group
can be bullied into retracting other peoples postings. 

1.00 release notes (15/07/94)

Hi there,

This is the very first release of this fresh and unstable Zmodem implementation.

The original licensing terms for this release have been superseded by the
BSD 2-Clause License above.

The reason this code and the corresponding documentation were created is
dissatisfaction with the current public domain versions of zmodem and their
documentation. As of this writing the code runs on exactly ONE operating 
system and you are very likely never to have heard of it. (Unite!; runs on
the 386 and better; microkernel etc..) So, this is likely not a plug and
play package but with some help from the world out there and xs4all
(the public internet access point in Amsterdam, The Netherlands) we can
turn it in to one. If someone would like to do a port; has a bug report or
would like to send us a message use any of the possibilities below.
Enjoy it and on to the next release

             Jacques Mattheij.
